[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124473,
      "id": "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "728ca42a-17f9-5348-b743-e8a6bbb0aa03",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124473/plots/plot-486c4f5718d4d225631cfeb36f9693e0b97d9742da5dc298489e737a23406658.png)\n\nWhich listed domain retains every input outside the constant segment and is maximal by inclusion among the listed domains on which $f(x)$ is invertible?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On $x\\in [2,5]$ the graph is constant, so we can retain only one point at output $y=0$.\n\n- On $x\\in [-1,2]\\cup (5,8]$ the left piece has output at most zero and the right piece has output greater than zero. Each piece is one-to-one, and their ranges do not overlap.\n\n- Adding any excluded plateau point repeats the output zero. Every other listed domain either omits a nonconstant piece or fails the horizontal line test.\n\nTherefore, the correct listed domain is:\n\n$$\nx\\in [-1,2]\\cup (5,8]\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c5c18a40-ccdb-55cd-9b16-59830c6a1c52",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "0139dc1f-6c51-5ecc-bad5-342ba5da15b2",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "x\\in [-1,2]\\cup (5,8]"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "90cf48a3-9ea7-537d-89a4-60b6fce14ce8",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "x\\in [-1,2)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "e7e264fb-d6b5-52ee-9b49-fe2f2ef35d0f",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "x\\in [-1,8]"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "cc4a9c53-34be-5bf0-b5f1-7c9551257b6b",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "x\\in [2,8]"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "3e95309d-0ecc-5be5-b2c6-c8cf37943700",
