  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124473,
      "id": "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d8e4826a-f11e-55bf-aa31-9324263b9c56",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124473/plots/plot-c58def5d18244b83b2ef30536fc5fdbfee3c1d8f2b375818bfca008c8297288d.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in (-3,-1]$\n\nII. $x\\in (-3,3]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-6,-3]\\cup [3,6)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in (-3,-1]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in (-3,3]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in [-6,-3]\\cup [3,6)$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8534551b-cf44-5b59-b28b-a882688ad762",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
