      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124473/plots/plot-bcd90b631446d56a29818eaeec452c5c34b6a2e4902dc69dda72d406d17f7f9e.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in (-4,-1]$\n\nII. $x\\in (-4,3]$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-7,-4]\\cup [3,6]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in (-4,-1]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in (-4,3]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in (-7,-4]\\cup [3,6]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
