    "question": {
      ":question/id": "793d86cf-f015-5150-86db-0133e4217ab3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124473/plots/plot-326c19ad1017fab8f7bec897ae7beb9c754f0c3b0a1e37aa3b149062ac2942b5.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in [-7,-3]\\cup [1,5)$\n\nII. $x\\in (-3,-1]$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-3,1]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in [-7,-3]\\cup [1,5)$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in (-3,-1]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in (-3,1]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
