  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124473,
      "id": "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e3563614-d32d-5a65-80a1-99f2fe16f895",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124473/plots/plot-65de7452dc61058a4379001259f74ecc549306e035aa84a7f557563227c9b900.png)\n\nWhich listed interval is greatest by inclusion among the listed intervals on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-2,2]$.\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function does not pass the horizontal line test on $x\\in [0,2]$. The horizontal line $y=0$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n- The function is invertible on $x\\in [-2,0]$. On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\nTherefore, the greatest listed interval on which the function is invertible is:\n\n$$\nx\\in [-2,0]\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f70e2ea2-115e-59a2-9e0d-c9c19d13ee9e",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
