  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124473,
      "id": "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "661f2203-a1e5-5e5d-86e8-339797b199ca",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124473/plots/plot-2412b1b38fd878d279c6bcc443c5c7e8ee024f7cae4a52737ea5d23672150e7f.png)\n\nWhich listed domain retains every input outside the constant segment and is maximal by inclusion among the listed domains on which $f(x)$ is invertible?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On $x\\in [1,3]$ the graph is constant, so we can retain only one point at output $y=0$.\n\n- On $x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5]$ the left piece has output at most zero and the right piece has output greater than zero. Each piece is one-to-one, and their ranges do not overlap.\n\n- Adding any excluded plateau point repeats the output zero. Every other listed domain either omits a nonconstant piece or fails the horizontal line test.\n\nTherefore, the correct listed domain is:\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5]\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8f1fe11d-bfdb-5c7b-aba1-03cd6a3f398a",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
